        {
          "entity": {
            "entity_id": "01a161a5-712d-725e-848c-300df80f5749",
            "entity_type": "panel",
            "name": "VDR MAX",
            "dimensions": {
              "tap.graph": "web"
            }
          },
          "node": {
            "name": "VDR MAX",
            "slug": "playground-vdr-max-intro",
            "description": "The page title and the method in one paragraph.",
            "view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel.html",
            "config": {
              "hide_header": false,
              "text": "FedRAMP's vulnerability rules ask a provider to evaluate every detected vulnerability for how likely it is to be exploited, whether it can be reached from the internet and how much it matters to agencies, and set deadlines from the answers. VDR max is the method that starts from the worst answer for every vulnerability and lowers an answer only when evidence is attached. An unevaluated vulnerability is therefore never mistaken for a safe one. Everything below is computed live from this grid; no deadline is stored."
            },
            "editor_view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel_editor.html"
          }
        },
        {
          "entity": {
            "entity_id": "01a161a5-712d-725e-848c-300e221ee314",
            "entity_type": "panel",
            "name": "The Rules",
            "dimensions": {
              "tap.graph": "web"
            }
          },
          "node": {
            "name": "The Rules",
            "slug": "playground-vdr-max-rules",
            "description": "What the method assumes and when it lets go.",
            "view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel.html",
            "config": {
              "hide_header": false,
              "text": "Every open finding that concerns a vulnerability gets an evaluation under the scheme max, with every factor unset.\nAn unset factor reads as the worst case: likely exploitable, internet-reachable, PAIN N5, and the strictest FedRAMP class (D).\nA factor counts as set only with evidence: an evidence edge from the evaluation, the finding citing the same evidence, and an evaluator and time recorded. Anything else reads as unset.\nPAIN cannot be lowered in this version, because no basis for lowering it has been agreed.\nSetting a factor never removes a deadline: the deadline is a bound counted from first detection with the worst-case row, never later than the exact one."
            },
            "editor_view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel_editor.html"
          }
        },
        {
          "entity": {
            "entity_id": "01a161a5-712d-725e-848c-300f26a3f0b5",
            "entity_type": "panel",
            "name": "From Finding To Deadline",
            "dimensions": {
              "tap.graph": "web"
            }
          },
          "node": {
            "name": "From Finding To Deadline",
            "slug": "playground-vdr-max-steps",
            "description": "The steps, and what does each on the system.",
            "view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel.html",
            "config": {
              "hide_header": false,
              "text": "1. A scanner finding concerns a vulnerability and is open.\n2. The VDR max collector gives it one evaluation (scheme max) with every factor unset, and never overwrites one a person has set.\n3. The effective factors are read: unset means worst case; a set factor counts only with evidence.\n4. The FedRAMP timeframe rows (seeded as data) give the hours for the class, level and exposure column: 12 hours at class D, N5, likely exploitable and reachable.\n5. The clock is counted from the finding's first detection: evaluation within 48 hours, remediation within 12 hours (the bound), acceptance after 192 days.\n6. A finding that is likely exploitable and reachable at N5 is a reportable incident under VER-TFR-IRI.\nRun by the vdr_max collector and the Searches vdr-deadlines, vdr-incidents and vdr-summary; computed on read."
            },
            "editor_view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel_editor.html"
          }
        },
